Subject: [PATCH 24/51] tei-v7 struct-27: Add a mandatory caller/consumer
 impact audit with an explici

---
 codemonkeys/prompts/editing_state_machine_prompts.py | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/codemonkeys/prompts/editing_state_machine_prompts.py b/codemonkeys/prompts/editing_state_machine_prompts.py
index 0c0f3ef..af85cb4 100644
--- a/codemonkeys/prompts/editing_state_machine_prompts.py
+++ b/codemonkeys/prompts/editing_state_machine_prompts.py
@@ -11,7 +11,7 @@ from codemonkeys.prompts.shared_state_machine_prompts import format_files
 FULL_FILE_EXAMPLE = """
 Before final approval, build a requirement-to-test matrix from the issue. For every independently stated behavior, record the implementation location, an ordinary-case test, a boundary or malformed-input test, and a regression test for the reported failure. Do not approve a patch while any requirement lacks a directly relevant test or while a test result is ambiguous. After each test run, map every failure back to the matrix and either revise the patch or explain why the failure is unrelated.
 
-Before final approval, inspect all callers and consumers of each modified symbol. Record the invariant each caller relies on, explain why the patch preserves compatible callers, and identify any changed error, ordering, mutation, or serialization behavior. Reopen implementation if any caller impact is unknown.
+Before final approval, enumerate every caller, subclass, override, serializer, parser, and public consumer of each modified symbol using repository search. For each consumer, record its expected inputs, outputs, exceptions, ordering, mutation, and compatibility invariant, then verify the patch against that record. If any consumer cannot be located or its behavior is unknown, do not approve the patch: search broader call paths, add a focused regression test, or revise the implementation. Treat changed error types, defaults, ordering, mutation, and serialization as behavior changes requiring explicit justification.
 
 When comparing alternative patches, first create a table with one row per issue requirement and one column per candidate. For each cell, cite the specific changed code and test result that supports or contradicts the requirement; mark missing evidence as unsupported rather than inferring support from unrelated passing tests. Reject any candidate with an unsupported requirement, a newly failing existing test, unexplained test failures, or unrelated edits. Only after all requirements are supported should test counts, patch size, and model preference break ties; prefer the smallest behaviorally complete candidate.
 
-- 
2.39.5 (Apple Git-154)

